;; The STARTER CATALOGUE — what a fresh graphden's marketplace lists before
;; anyone has published anything (docs/MARKETPLACE.md § 11).
;;
;; `graphden.packages.starter-catalogue/seed!` publishes every entry at boot
;; (init-key `:exec/starter-catalogue`, default ON) through the SAME row the
;; publish route writes (`registry-shared/version-row`): in the platform
;; tier, so each is `public? true` / `status approved` and listed at once.
;; Keyed by `(name, version)` — an entry that already exists is skipped, so a
;; restart never duplicates and a withdrawn version stays withdrawn until
;; the catalogue bumps it. To change a listing, bump its `:version`: rows
;; are immutable (the old version stays, as a publish would leave it).
;;
;; Entry shapes (the listing fields are the publish route's):
;;   theme  — {:kind "theme"  :name :version :description :category :tags :payload}
;;            payload = {:mode :tokens :fonts :scale} (docs/MARKETPLACE.md § 5);
;;            token names are the editor's allow-list (`gdThemeTokens`,
;;            editor-prefs.js), values colour literals — the apply path
;;            sanitises, `starter-catalogue-test` pins the names.
;;   keymap — {:kind "keymap" :name :version :description :category :tags :payload}
;;            payload = {:bindings {id {:keys :leader}}} over the ids
;;            `editor-shortcuts.js` registers (§ 6).
;;   fns    — {:kind "fns" :name :version :description :category :tags
;;             :ns-root :dependencies :fns}
;;            `:fns` is fns.edn syntax (each def's `:namespace` defaults to
;;            `:ns-root`); `:dependencies` names the fns referenced from
;;            OUTSIDE the bundle (what `:export-namespace` would compute) —
;;            install refuses when one is missing. Every fns entry is
;;            installed and executed by `starter-catalogue-test`.
;;
;; Names carry the `graphden.` / `starter.` prefix on purpose: a public
;; name is registry-wide and first come first served (§ 2), so the
;; platform's own listings never squat a name a user would pick.
